==Trivia==
*In [[Nyanko Daisensou|BCJP]], this sub-chapter contains many puns and references that were lost in translation:
**Candy Paradise's Japanese name, Karume Tōgenkyō (カルメ桃源郷, "Karume Utopia"), is derived from ''karumeyaki'' (カルメやき), the Japanese word for [[wikipedia:Honeycomb toffee|honeycomb toffee]].
**[[Soybean Graveyard|Kinako Bochi]] (きなこ墓地, "Kinako Cemetery"), sounds similar to kinako mochi (きなこ餅), [[wikipedia:Mochi|mochi]] coated ''[[wikipedia:Kinako|kinako]]'' (roasted soybean flour). It could also be a reference to [https://japanese-products.blog/2018/04/11/kinakobo-japanese-dagashi-candy/ kinakobō] (きなこ棒), a stick-shaped candy made using ''kinako''.
**[[Pointed Lakeshore|Tongari Kohan]] (トンガリ湖畔, "Tongari Lakeside"), sounds similar to Tongari Kōn (とんがりコーン), the Japanese name of [[wikipedia:Bugles (snack)|Bugles]]. ''Tongari'' means pointed, hence its English name being Pointed Lakeshore.
**[[Candy Downpour|Furisosogu Neri Ame]] (降り注ぐねりあめ, "Raining Neri Ame") references the candy [https://japanese-products.blog/2019/02/13/neri-ame/ Neri Ame].
**[[Yogurt Archipelago|Yogu Rettō]] (ヨーグ列島, "Yogurt Archipelago") is a pun on ''yōguruto'' (ヨーグルト, yogurt) and ''rettō'' (列島, "archipelago").
**[[Cookie Cutter Ruins|Katanuki Iseki]] (かたぬき遺跡, "Katanuki Ruins") refers to the candy-carving festival activity ''[[wikipedia:Katanuki|katanuki]]''.
**[[Pop-pop Wonderland|Pachi Pachi Wonderland]] (パチパチワンダーランド) may be a reference to [https://japanese-products.blog/2021/05/06/pachi-pachi-panic/ Pachi Pachi Panic] (パチパチパニック, ''Pachi Pachi Panikku''), a candy similar to [[wikipedia:Pop Rocks|Pop Rocks]].

